<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Evening Routine</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Brush My Teeth.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>Reading: Summarize a text using a graphic organizer.<br>Reading: Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs.<br>Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>Reading: Can summarize bedtime routines in a graphic organizer (e.g., "First, I put on my pajamas. Then, I brush my teeth. Last, I go to bed.").<br>Can outline main ideas of bedtime stories with simple keywords (e.g., "sleep," "brush," and "night").<br>Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words related to daily routines (e.g., /wɔːʃ/ in the word "wash" and /t-iːθ/ in the word "teeth").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge, and introduce the context for the lesson through teacher modeling and clarification of new language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of various evening routine actions (e.g., brush teeth, wash face, put on pajamas, read a book, go to bed). The teacher asks, "What do you do at night?" and points to flashcards, eliciting one-word answers or short phrases like "brush teeth" or "go to bed" from students. This activates vocabulary from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models short sentences about an evening routine using sequencing words and actions. "First, I brush my teeth." (Teacher mimes brushing teeth). "Then, I put on my pajamas." (Teacher mimes putting on pajamas). "Last, I go to bed." (Teacher mimes going to sleep). The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /b/ in "bed" and /t/ in "teeth", and demonstrates blending sounds for /w-ɒ-ʃ/ ("wash") and /t-iːθ/ ("teeth") by saying the sounds slowly and then together.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and sequencing.
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I brush my teeth in the morning or at night?" (Expected answer: "At night!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'first' the beginning or the end?" (Expected answer: "The beginning!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___<br> (5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>This stage introduces new language content through a reading text, connected to the scenario "My Evening Routine," focusing on reading comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario "My Evening Routine":</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated narrative text titled "My Bedtime Story" on a large chart, projector, or individual worksheets. The text describes a simple evening routine using sequencing words (First, Then, Last) and target vocabulary (brush, teeth, pajamas, wash, face, story, bed, sleepy, goodnight).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated narrative text. The genre is a simple descriptive story. The register is informal, suitable for a child's perspective. The format is a short paragraph or a sequence of 3-4 simple sentences with accompanying pictures for visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence (from MEDUCA):</b> Comprehending simple descriptive texts related to personal daily routines.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher reads the title and asks, "What is this story about?" (Expected: "A bedtime routine" or "What I do at night"). The teacher reads the text aloud once, pointing to words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Picture Matching:</b> Students listen to the teacher read the story again. They are given a worksheet with 3-4 pictures representing key actions from the story (e.g., brushing teeth, putting on pajamas, reading a book, sleeping). Students read the text independently and number the pictures in the correct order as they appear in the story.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Keyword Identification:</b> After reading, the teacher asks students to circle or underline three keywords in the text that tell them what the boy does before sleeping (e.g., "brush," "pajamas," "read").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___<br> (10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>This stage focuses on developing accuracy in reading, applying the target grammar and vocabulary from the text through structured activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Sequencing words ("First, then, next, last"), Present simple for daily routines ("I brush my teeth."), Time and status expressions ("It's bedtime.", "I am sleepy.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> nouns: bed, pajamas, teeth, toothbrush, story, book, night, water, face; verbs: brush, wash, wear, sleep, read; adjectives: sleepy, tired, clean.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /b/ in "bed" and /t/ in "teeth". Phonemic awareness: Blending /w-ɒ-ʃ/ in "wash" and /t-iː-θ/ in "teeth".</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Reading & Fill-in-the-Blanks):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the story, but with key sequencing words or vocabulary words missing. They read the sentences and fill in the blanks using a word bank provided (e.g., "First, I ______ my teeth. Then, I put on my _______. Last, I go to ______."). The word bank includes "brush," "pajamas," "bed," "First," "Then," "Last."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Reordering):</b> The teacher provides strips of paper with jumbled parts of sentences describing a routine (e.g., "my teeth / brush / First, I"). In pairs, students read the words and work together to reorder them to form correct sentences. This helps them understand sentence structure while focusing on reading individual words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Reading & Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A reads one of their completed fill-in-the-blank sentences from activity 1. Partner B listens and verifies if it matches the original story or makes sense. They then switch roles. This activity reinforces reading accuracy and builds confidence. The teacher circulates, listening for correct use of target vocabulary and pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___<br> (15-18 min)</p>
            </td>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Students use the acquired language in a communicative task, applying their reading comprehension to summarize information in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario:</b> Students will summarize the evening routine from the text using a graphic organizer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual or Pair Work. Students receive a blank graphic organizer worksheet with three boxes labeled "First," "Then," and "Last." They reread the story from Stage 2. Their task is to identify the main action for each sequence word and either draw a simple picture representing it or write keywords/short phrases from the text into the corresponding boxes. For example, under "First," they might draw a toothbrush and teeth or write "brush teeth." Under "Then," they might draw pajamas or write "wear pajamas." Under "Last," they might draw a bed or write "go to bed" / "sleep."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "First, I Brush My Teeth." and Scenario: "My Evening Routine":</b> The graphic organizer directly represents the sequence of actions in the "My Evening Routine" scenario as presented in the text, reinforcing the theme of "First, I Brush My Teeth."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in understanding and extracting main ideas from a text, collaborative interaction if working in pairs, and accurate completion of the graphic organizer as a summary tool.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___<br> (8-10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>This stage involves formative assessment to check learning and allows students to reflect on their own progress.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and informal peer assessment (if done in pairs).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students show their completed graphic organizers. The teacher may ask a few students to briefly describe one part of their routine from the graphic organizer orally (e.g., "What do you do first?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate summary: Do the pictures/keywords in the graphic organizer correctly reflect the main actions from the text?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of sequencing words: Are "First, Then, Last" used correctly to organize the routine?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary: Are key vocabulary words (e.g., brush, teeth, pajamas, bed) present and used appropriately?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity: Is the graphic organizer clear and easy to understand?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Optional) Intelligible pronunciation: If students speak, are key words like "brush" and "teeth" pronounced clearly, demonstrating phonemic awareness?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper or verbally:
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I tell my friend three things I do in my evening routine from the story?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word I learned or practiced today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___<br> (5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>This final stage encourages both student and teacher reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and provides a bridge to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to read in the story today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult for me, and how can I practice it more?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What reading strategies worked best for 3rd graders (e.g., picture matching, graphic organizers)? Were the target vocabulary and sequencing words understood and applied in the graphic organizers? Was the phonemic awareness practice (/w-ɒ-ʃ/, /t-iːθ/) effective during modeling and practice? What adjustments are needed for Lesson 3, especially regarding preparing students to orally communicate their routines using the learned structures?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on reading and understanding an evening routine. Lesson 3 will build on this by having students *speak* and *interact* about their own or a simple routine, using the sequencing words and vocabulary practiced in this reading lesson. Students will use the graphic organizers from this lesson as a scaffold for their speaking task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___<br> (2-3 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>